summary:
Short answer: it can be done, but it doesn't work today, and the gaps are specific.

(1) **Processing.** python/server.py runs fine on its own: it has no Electron dependency, creates its own token, fixes PATH and serves the UI. It can take a recording all the way to notes if two calls are made in order: `voice_memo_pipeline.py --once` (copies the file from inbox/ into recordings/ and creates the DB row), then `POST /api/process-recording {id}` (transcription, speaker labels and LLM notes, run in the request thread). Nothing makes those two calls automatically except Electron's stopRecording path (main.js:714/772 -> runArchivePipelineWithRetry -> diarizeLatestRecording). The repo ships no cron job, plist or file watcher. cron_runner.py is only started by main.js. /api/scan only restarts an optional "Cora Scanner.app" LaunchAgent that isn't configured. So even with Electron running, a file dropped into inbox/ waits until the next meeting recording stops. diarizeLatestRecording also processes only the single newest unprocessed row (db.py:463 LIMIT 1), so several uploads in one scan would leave the rest unprocessed.

(2) **Upload.** There is no upload endpoint. do_POST always calls read_json first (http.py:131), which requires application/json and caps bodies at 1 MB (http.py:64-71). Audio upload needs its own branch before that call: stream the body to disk in chunks, then rename it into inbox/.

(3) **Auth.** tailscale serve keeps the browser's Host header. I confirmed this in Tailscale's serve.go: `r.Out.Host = r.In.Host`; it also adds X-Forwarded-Host, X-Forwarded-Proto and X-Forwarded-For. So every remote request gets a 403 "Forbidden host" (http.py:40-42). The Origin check would also reject `https://<name>.ts.net`, because it only strips `http://` (http.py:43-46). The only token is a single per-launch token that changes on every start (auth.py:33). Electron sets it as a cookie only for 127.0.0.1, so the phone has no way to get it.

(4) **UI.** The UI half-works at phone width (bottom nav at ≤820px). But every API call depends on the Electron cookie, and the fallback buttons that run when Electron isn't there would start or stop the Mac Studio's own microphone capture or open Finder on it.

(5) **Phone audio.** Single-track iPhone m4a goes through cleanly:
- the denoise step and the mixed playback copy are skipped;
- the mic/system mix is skipped;
- every segment is labeled "You" (or the user's name).

That's correct for a solo voice note. An in-person conversation recorded on the phone would also come out as all "You": the local path has no way to tell voices apart.

**What's missing:**
- an upload route;
- a background worker in the server process that archives then processes the specific upload, with a single-flight lock (none exists today);
- an allowlist for the exact ts.net host and origin;
- long-lived per-device tokens with scopes and a deny-by-default route list;
- a small mobile page;
- a launchd plist;
- handling for the port clash with Electron.
